From 0ae89dffb6a35b805a649274347435fbb600de5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: liviyifan <344781483+liviyifan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 21:35:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] [AArch64] GNU ILP32: fold small offsets of wrapping
 addresses

A GEP without inbounds computes its address modulo 2^32, so a load or store
uses zext(Base + C) and cannot use an immediate offset: "add wN, wM, #C"
then "[xN]". Use zext(Base) + C instead when C is a constant of less than a
page, also for zext(Base + C1) + C2 when the outer offset (for example from
narrowing a load to the byte holding a flag) does not have the opposite sign
and |C1 + C2| is less than a page. The addresses differ only when Base is
within |C1| of 0 or 2^32: for C1 > 0 both are unmapped on Linux (the first
page, and the space above the 4 GiB address space of an ILP32 process), so
both accesses fault; for C1 < 0 Base is in the first page and not a valid
pointer. GCC folds such offsets the same way.

The fold is done on Linux only, for simple unindexed loads and stores, when
extending Base is free or replaces the 32-bit address computation. It can
be disabled with -aarch64-ilp32-fold-address-offsets=false.

Such GEPs come from LSR, from ArgumentPromotion (at -O3 it moves field
loads to the callers without inbounds) and from InstCombine. With
-fno-strict-overflow (pointer wrap) every GEP is one.

SQLite amalgamation, -O2 -fwrapv: 235579 -> 234151 instructions (ILP32 vs
LP64 gap 2.04% -> 1.43%); with -fno-strict-overflow 278803 -> 237215.
CPython 3.14 (-O3, tail-calling interpreter): 731977 -> 719029 instructions.

Signed-off-by: liviyifan <344781483+liviyifan@users.noreply.github.com>
